# Exercise: roll
#
# The `roll` word rotates `n + 1` items, bringing the item at
# depth `n` (0-indexed from the top) up to the new top.
#
#   0 roll = does nothing            (rotates 1 item)
#   1 roll = swap                    (rotates 2 items)
#   2 roll = rot                     (rotates 3 items)
#
# Stack effect: ( ..values n -- ..rotated )
#
# Example:
#   1 2 3 4  2 roll    # rotates top 3 items: depth-2 is 2,
#                      # so 2 moves to top: ( 1 4 3 2 )
#
# Your task: with `10 20 30 40` on the stack, use `roll` to
# bring the 10 to the top. Count from 0: 40 is at depth 0,
# 30 at depth 1, 20 at depth 2, 10 at depth 3.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-roll ( -- )
    10 20 30 40
    # Your code here - use roll with the right count
    # Do not edit below this line
    10 test.assert-eq
    40 test.assert-eq
    30 test.assert-eq
    20 test.assert-eq
;
